The SM7150's CSIPHY clocks were incomplete and misaligned

WHAT HAPPENS
------------
The SM7150's four CSIPHYs asked for clock lists that differed from one another
and none of them complete:

    CSIPHY0   4 clocks   cphy_rx_src, csiphy0, csiphy0_timer_src, csiphy0_timer
    CSIPHY1   2 clocks   csiphy1, csiphy1_timer
    CSIPHY2   2 clocks   csiphy2, csiphy2_timer
    CSIPHY3   4 clocks   csiphy3, csiphy3_timer_src, csiphy3_timer, cphy_rx_src

The sdm845 -which carries the same 3ph CSIPHY and DOES capture on mainline- asks
for eight on all four: camnoc_axi, soc_ahb, slow_ahb_src, cpas_ahb, cphy_rx_src,
csiphyN, csiphyN_timer_src and csiphyN_timer.

So the SM7150 was missing camnoc_axi, slow_ahb_src, cpas_ahb, cphy_rx_src and
csiphyN_timer_src. (soc_ahb does not exist in the SM7150's camcc; its equivalent
is cpas_ahb, which is there.)

The VFE and the CSID bring them up as a side effect, so WHILE it transmits they
are on -checked by reading clk_summary while running-. But the CSIPHY needs them
ALREADY when it powers itself up and reads its version register, which goes over
the AHB bus. And in one test that register returned

    CSIPHY 3PH HW Version = 0x00000000

when the normal value is 0x40010000. A register that reads zero is the classic
symptom of a bus with no clock.

AND ON CSIPHY3, THE RATES WERE MISALIGNED AS WELL
-------------------------------------------------
clock_rate is paired by POSITION with clock. On CSIPHY3:

    clock       = { "csiphy3", "csiphy3_timer_src", "csiphy3_timer", "cphy_rx_src" }
    clock_rate  = { {0},       {300000000},         {0},             {384000000, ...} }

The only rate actually used is the one for "csiphy3_timer" -it is the one that
matches by name in rate_set, with an exact strcmp- and it had {0}, while the
384/400 MHz ended up on "cphy_rx_src", which takes no rate at all.

The SM7150 device tree already declares the seven names for the four PHYs, so
this needs no changes outside the driver.
